WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:07.480
What’s up Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:10.599
Convicted serial killer Ted Bundy is HOT right
now.

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:15.400
And, uh, if you find that sentence as weird
as I do, then stick around.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.390
Thanks to the recent Netflix documentary series
and the clumsily titled Netflix movie 'Extremely

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:25.870
Wicked, Shockingly Evil &amp; Vile' by the same
director, suddenly everyone’s talking 'bout

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:26.870
Bundy.

00:00:26.870 --> 00:00:31.609
And not just in a 'wow, what an awful person'
way, but in a 'that Ted Bundy was Hot' kinda

00:00:31.609 --> 00:00:32.609
way.

00:00:32.609 --> 00:00:37.210
So hot, in fact, the actor cast to portray
him was two-time MTV 'Best Shirtless Performance'

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:39.350
award winner, Zac Efron.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.590
After watching the film though we were left
wondering exactly what the movie was trying

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:44.590
to say.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:49.629
That’s because it makes a weird omission
for a film about a serial killer: actual murder.

00:00:49.629 --> 00:00:54.620
It makes a strong stylistic choice to deprive
the audience of any scenes that show him murdering

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:57.370
his victims until its final moments.

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:01.149
For most of its runtime, it seems like the
film is asking us to make up our own mind

00:01:01.149 --> 00:01:04.239
about a dude CONVICTED of murdering a bunch
of people.

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:06.380
Also, there was some necrophilia.

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:10.350
And with the casting of the undeniably handsome
Efron, it feels like the film is trying to

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:13.820
make some sort of statement about appearances
and what they hide.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:15.520
This all seems kinda weird, right?

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.390
A movie about a serial killer that spends
the vast majority of its running time not

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:24.130
on the murder stuff but building an image
of a charming guy defending his innocence?

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:27.640
What is the movie actually saying about Bundy,
or serial killers?

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.260
Is it about the nature of evil?

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:30.790
Is it saying anything at all?

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:35.350
Let’s find out in this Wisecrack Edition
on “Extremely Wicked, Shockingly… whatever.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:37.040
The Zac Efron Ted Bundy Movie.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:02:32.680
And spoiler: Ted Bundy did it.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.020
Alright guys, first, a

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:39.280
quick recap: Smokin' hot Law student Theodore
Bundy meets secretary Liz Kendall at a bar

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.450
where they hit it off and start dating.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:46.190
Not long after, handsome Ted gets arrested
as a suspect in the murders of multiple young

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.220
women from the Seattle area.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.810
Despite being initially released, and insisting
to Liz he is innocent, Ted and his ripped

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:57.220
abs are soon arrested again and put in jail
to await trial.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:58.540
But he escapes!

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:03.440
Only to get caught a week later and sent back
to prison, still protesting his innocence.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:08.520
He escapes AGAIN but this time makes it all
the way to Florida where - suspiciously enough

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.040
- more women get assaulted and murdered.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.920
He’s put back in jail, yet again, but this
time he finally makes it to trial.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.459
Somehow he manages to find time to get a woman
pregnant, and marry her in court

00:03:19.459 --> 00:03:27.319
“will you marry me” “your honor” “yes”
and then get found guilty and sentenced to

00:03:27.319 --> 00:03:28.680
the electric chair.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:32.569
Throughout this whole story Ted's partner
Liz clings to the hope that he was innocent,

00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:36.550
despite being the one who initially phoned
him in to the police.

00:03:36.550 --> 00:03:38.120
“Ted.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.120
Ted Bundy.”

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:42.830
The movie ends with Liz demanding to know
the truth from sweet Teddy Boy, who writes

00:03:42.830 --> 00:03:47.920
'Hacksaw' on the prison glass and that counts
as an admission I guess, prompting a flashback

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:49.799
to one of his many murders.

00:03:49.799 --> 00:03:54.610
We then see some of the real life news footage
of scenes depicted in the movie over the credits.

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:55.620
The End.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:58.950
Even just from that brief summary, you kinda
get the feel for how much the movie leaves

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:02.130
out and how much it enjoys having Efron as
a lead.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:06.819
Throughout the film we are forced to pay attention
to how Bundy looks and how handsome he is,

00:04:06.819 --> 00:04:10.180
and not necessarily what the character has
or hasn't done.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:14.780
Purely from the fact Teen Choice 'Male Hottie'
award winner Zac Efron portrays Bundy, you

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:18.359
know we are being encouraged to buy into Bundy's
attractiveness.

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:22.790
Even beyond the casting, the movie pushes
the idea of 'Hot Bundy' with the way the movie

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:23.960
sees the character.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:27.840
Some of the first shots of the film are women
gazing coyly and seductively at the camera

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:29.360
from Bundy's point of view.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:33.400
When he escapes for the first time, he stops
regularly along the street to wink and smile

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:35.520
at women who all reciprocate.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:39.110
Several women are seen being interviewed who
say how cute he is and immediately conflate

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:40.710
that with how he must be innocent.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:41.710
“I’m not afraid of him.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:44.370
He just doesn’t seem like the type to kill
somebody.”

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:45.700
And, well... wouldn't you?!

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:46.980
I mean, come on!

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.470
Look at that smile!

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:51.780
Even the way Efron is filmed makes you desire
the Character of Bundy.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:56.920
We see Efron both semi-nude and butt naked
as well as multiple sex scenes either repeatedly

00:04:56.920 --> 00:04:59.960
flashed back to or lingered on in the moment.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:04.650
What we're saying is: This movie wants to
bang Zac Efron and it wants you to as well,

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:07.230
which I believe is known as “laying a thirst
trap”.

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.889
If we're being generous this could be what's
smart about the film.

00:05:10.889 --> 00:05:14.890
Even Bundy’s judge said how sad it was that
such a bright young man was going to the electric

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:21.190
chair “It's a tragedy to look forth and
see such a total waste, I believe, of humanity

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:23.080
that I've witnessed in this court.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:24.370
You're a bright young man.”

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:29.120
The reason we even know that fact is because
his was one of the first televised court cases,

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:31.919
mostly because Bundy was so unassuming and
handsome.

00:05:31.919 --> 00:05:35.699
The movie could be trying to depict him as
a central figure in the move toward docu-drama

00:05:35.699 --> 00:05:38.570
style news reporting that we’re so familiar
with today.

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:42.700
Whether it’s the OJ Simpson’s trial, or
the Serial Podcast, Bundy’s attractiveness

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:47.070
can be seen as patient zero for this move
toward sensationalism in media.

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:51.280
If we're to understand this reaction to Bundy,
we need to see him as handsome and who better

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:54.570
to accomplish that than High School Musical
Heartthrob Zac Efron?

00:05:54.570 --> 00:06:00.300
Well, maybe, but to understand why his casting
and the way he's shot makes us uncomfortable,

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:05.120
we need to dip into some philosophy- specifically,
the philosophy of aesthetics.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:09.100
Aesthetics is a pretty broad term but it can
be briefly summarized as the philosophy of

00:06:09.100 --> 00:06:10.100
art.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:14.080
The word gets its modern usage from Alexander
Baumgarten who’s text ‘Aesthetica’,

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:19.770
a word which had - until then - meant “Sensation”,
redefined it to mean a study of “good”

00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:21.860
and “bad” taste regarding art and beauty.

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:26.410
Typical of Enlightenment thinking, Baumgarten
was trying to apply principles of logic and

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:28.720
reason to subjective choices.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.740
Today the study of aesthetics has branched
out to almost everything beyond art, to the

00:06:32.740 --> 00:06:36.240
point where my haircut and t-shirts are aesthetic
choices.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:40.759
It’s all about subjective things like beauty,
taste and appearances.

00:06:40.759 --> 00:06:45.260
In film, aesthetics can not only be about
it’s appearance in the traditional sense,

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:50.699
but the variety of choices that we might call
artistic: Think framing, points of view, editing,

00:06:50.699 --> 00:06:55.130
costumes, the music, or how this all congeals
into a mood.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:59.050
Aesthetics is a big part of the Bundy story
because they both deal with the subjective

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:04.419
appraisal of certain things, especially things
we think of as being 'Beautiful'.

00:07:04.419 --> 00:07:09.039
Beyond casting a sex-icon as Bundy, the film
makes a series of aesthetic choices to play

00:07:09.039 --> 00:07:10.280
up his appeal.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:15.090
It's well shot with naturalistic lighting,
uses traditionally attractive framing, and

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.100
makes use of sympathetic film grain.

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:20.930
That is to say, it’s not grungy like Saw,
but visually appealing.

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:25.819
The production and costume design is certainly
period correct, but the characters, especially

00:07:25.819 --> 00:07:30.930
Bundy, are wearing expensive and fashionable
clothes not just for the time but for today

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:31.930
too.

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:33.389
Love that sweater, Zac.

00:07:33.389 --> 00:07:38.060
Combine that with the retrofilm stock moments,
like the 8mm 'home-movie' footageand the whole

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:41.060
thing has the appearance of an extended Instagram
post.

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:45.470
But deeper than that, the almost total lack
of violence in the movie feels like an Aesthetic

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:46.539
choice too.

00:07:46.539 --> 00:07:52.289
Some of Bundy's murders are described in court
“Strangled with a pantyhose garrote so tight

00:07:52.289 --> 00:07:56.340
that her neck was constricted to half its
size.”but we don't see much more than the

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:58.780
occasional photograph of bloodstains.

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:03.530
Other than the last five minutes, Bundy is
not shown harming anyone, and when he is,

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:08.139
the effectiveness of the scene is driven by
the sound design of that hacksaw, not the

00:08:08.139 --> 00:08:16.180
visuals
Extremely Wicked is aesthetically driven,

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:20.960
just like – the movie would argue – the
trial and legacy of Bundy is itself.

00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:25.770
Except there's that Goethe quote at the start:
“Few people have the imagination for reality.”

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:31.750
This quote implies the horrors the real life
Bundy enacted should not, or cannot, be imagined.

00:08:31.750 --> 00:08:36.390
Yet by removing them almost entirely, is the
movie asking us to imagine them?

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:39.590
This is what people were forced to do with
the real Bundy at the time, so the film-makers

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:42.390
make the aesthetic choice to keep his violence
largely hidden.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:46.709
If it’s not asking us to imagine his crimes,
then should we just forget them?

00:08:46.709 --> 00:08:50.709
If the movie withholds a sense of disgust
and we come away from it thinking Ted Bundy

00:08:50.709 --> 00:08:54.040
was more sexy than horrific, is that okay?

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:58.519
What responsibility do filmmakers have to
convince us that bad people are bad?

00:08:58.519 --> 00:09:03.620
This discussion on the ‘Aesthetic of Evil’
and the intersection of aesthetics and ethics

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:06.899
is something society has struggled with throughout
history.

00:09:06.899 --> 00:09:11.950
From the Devil in Milton's Paradise Lost,
to Nazis wearing Hugo Boss, this idea of bad

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:17.520
things being beautiful has always troubled
us, so how do we best represent that in art?

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:20.230
For example, consider the movie American Psycho.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:25.120
Our protagonist, or the guy we’re supposed
to relate to, is a serial killer.

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:29.140
We actually kind of like Patrick Bateman and
want him to get what he wants.

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:33.850
But the film, under the watchful eye of director
Mary Harron, and with a masterful performance

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:37.801
by Christian Bale, is able to achieve a tone
in which we’re convinced that we’re not

00:09:37.801 --> 00:09:41.130
supposed to take Patrick Bateman entirely
seriously.

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:43.529
This is the “aesthetic” of the film.

00:09:43.529 --> 00:09:47.790
So just because the hero of the movie is a
serial killer, the movie doesn't celebrate

00:09:47.790 --> 00:09:48.790
serial killing.

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:52.670
If it did, it would probably be a bad movie
that we would all hate.

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:57.060
This would be us making an ethical judgment
in response to the aesthetic choice.

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:01.390
This interplay between aesthetics and ethics
has been explored endlessly by philosophers

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:07.120
like Baumgarten, Immanuel Kant, Gilles Deleuze
and Jacques Ranciere - just to name a few.

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:12.529
But to figure out how to evaluate Efron’s
Bundy, we’re turning to theorist Mary Devereaux.

00:10:12.529 --> 00:10:17.390
In her essay 'Beauty and Evil', she analyses
Leni Riefenstahl’s infamous Nazi propaganda

00:10:17.390 --> 00:10:21.680
film Triumph of the Will and explains that
it used a lot of innovative techniques for

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:24.100
what was meant to be a documentary.

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:28.210
Riefenstahl used dynamic camera movements,
only seen in fiction movies of that era, by

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:31.540
training camera operators to shoot while wearing
roller-skates.

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:36.070
She dressed her crew in uniforms to hide them
amongst the crowds and shot over 60 hours

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:39.280
of footage that was edited together in dynamic
ways.

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:44.240
As Devereaux puts it “Riefenstahl's concerns
were aesthetic” which meant the end result

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:49.379
for the film is “A beautiful vision of Hitler
and New Germany that is morally repugnant”

00:10:49.379 --> 00:10:50.379
- Devereaux.

00:10:50.379 --> 00:10:54.050
If the film is beautifully made but with a
terrible message it should be possible to

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:56.200
separate those two factors.

00:10:56.200 --> 00:11:00.120
Devereaux defines this distinction between
the aesthetic appraisal of a work of art from

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:05.769
a moral one as 'Aesthetic Formalism', acknowledging
that these two considerations may pull in

00:11:05.769 --> 00:11:09.620
different directions and should therefore
be judged distinctly.

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:14.980
Or as she memorably puts it “[You can admire]
the beauty of a fog at sea, despite its danger.”

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:19.490
Except, Devereaux argues, that isn't, in fact,
possible.

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:21.769
Because admiring that fog will get you killed.

00:11:21.769 --> 00:11:26.459
A lot of Triumph of the Will's aesthetic principles
are tied to its content.

00:11:26.459 --> 00:11:32.040
Until her death, Riefenstahl insisted the
film was NOT propaganda, it was a documentary.

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:36.370
But we can't ignore that her creative choices
were overwhelmingly driven by a desire to

00:11:36.370 --> 00:11:39.160
make Hitler and the Nazis look appealing.

00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:49.230
Whether that's with interviews of people who
solely express positive views about Hitler.

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:53.570
admiring shots of the goose-stepping soldiers
or just the fact it was shot specifically

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:58.870
at a huge, grandiose, pro-Nazi rally with
hundreds of thousands of attendees, this is

00:11:58.870 --> 00:12:01.649
a movie about how popular the Nazis were.

00:12:01.649 --> 00:12:06.279
Devereaux explains that, ever since Plato
made the connection, society has always equated

00:12:06.279 --> 00:12:11.450
Beauty with Goodness, an idea that has long
been associated with the study of aesthetics.

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:15.930
But it is only in the relatively recent past
that we have moved away from this idea, largely

00:12:15.930 --> 00:12:20.649
because of fascist groups like the Nazis who
demanded racial purity and the resurrection

00:12:20.649 --> 00:12:22.480
of classical architecture.

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:27.370
The aesthetic of the modernist movement after
World War II resulted in very not-beautiful

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:32.350
things like Brutalist architecture and bold,
abstract graphic design to try and reconcile

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:37.860
the ugliness of a post-war world against the
Nazis desire for purity and beauty.

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:42.800
“Beauty is no longer central to art” as
Devereaux puts it, but art can certainly render

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:44.290
evil beautiful.

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:47.579
Because of that she says the best way to engage
with something like Triumph of the Will is

00:12:47.579 --> 00:12:53.930
not to separate its aesthetic principles from
its moral ones but to “Engage with its vision.”

00:12:53.930 --> 00:12:58.769
The 'vision' she refers to is what purpose
the work of art has and how it is presented.

00:12:58.769 --> 00:13:04.380
In film this means the story, themes AND aesthetic
should be critically read as a whole.

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:09.649
By this standard Riefenstahl's film is NOT
a good work of art, not in spite of its aesthetic

00:13:09.649 --> 00:13:11.760
choices but BECAUSE of them.

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:16.640
It is a film specifically designed to make
the Nazis, and the awfulness that went with

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:18.380
them, appealing.

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:22.490
Per Devereaux “There is a reason to withhold
the highest aesthetic praise from works that

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:27.410
present as beautiful, attractive and good,
what is, in fact, evil” - Devereaux.

00:13:27.410 --> 00:13:31.890
So if we want to understand how something
evil can be represented as beautiful and vice

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:35.610
versa, it's worth considering what the 'vision'
for Extremely Wicked is.

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:39.639
We're going to do that by comparing it to
another Ted Bundy biopic - there's a ton of

00:13:39.639 --> 00:13:43.920
them by the way, we just picked one out of
the hat – the aptly titled Ted Bundy from

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:45.029
2002.

00:13:45.029 --> 00:13:49.630
This movie is a much lower budget, much more
graphic, retelling of the Bundy story and

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:50.630
his crimes.

00:13:50.630 --> 00:13:55.139
It covers the same sort of time frame as the
Efron movie but goes all in on depicting many

00:13:55.139 --> 00:14:01.180
of Bundy's rapes and murders, and unlike Extremely
Wicked it's entirely disinterested with his

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:02.180
appearance.

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:04.970
A scene at the beginning where Bundy looks
into the mirror and rehearses his greeting

00:14:04.970 --> 00:14:05.970
“Hi there...

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:11.920
Hi there, my name's Ted, nice to meet you”
makes the point to the audience that his charm

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:15.760
and attractiveness is false and he has to
practice it.

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:20.360
He is seen as charismatic by some characters
but we also see him rubbing one out in a bush

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:22.779
which kinda takes the sheen off him.

00:14:22.779 --> 00:14:26.959
Beyond simply not wanting to make the character
attractive to the audience, this film's entire

00:14:26.959 --> 00:14:30.070
aesthetic is completely different than the
Efron movie.

00:14:30.070 --> 00:14:35.350
Its budget means the picture quality and cinematography
is nowhere near as crisp as Extremely Wicked,

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:40.040
the period details and costumes are paid lip
service but aren't as in depth, the violence

00:14:40.040 --> 00:14:45.930
is visceral and lingered on, and, unlike Efron's
depiction, at the end we see Bundy humiliated

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:49.260
in prison and die on-screen in a graphic way.

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:54.290
This low-budget, video-nasty aesthetic seems
more interested in showing us the horror of

00:14:54.290 --> 00:14:56.990
what Ted Bundy did and who he really was.

00:14:56.990 --> 00:15:02.980
As such the 'visions' for the two films, despite
telling the same story, couldn't be more different.

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:06.829
Extremely Wicked wants to tell Ted Bundy's
story but maintain an appealing aesthetic

00:15:06.829 --> 00:15:09.550
in much the same way as the real Bundy did.

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:13.699
The problem with this 'vision' for the movie,
i.e. the way its aesthetic works with its

00:15:13.699 --> 00:15:17.940
story and themes, is that it's more interested
in how it presents itself than in telling

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:20.240
you the REAL story of Ted Bundy.

00:15:20.240 --> 00:15:24.579
By focusing on the perspective of Liz Kendall’s
character and what she knows about Bundy,

00:15:24.579 --> 00:15:28.730
we spend most of the time just as confused
as her as to how we should feel about him.

00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:32.770
It’s also really uneven, in that it doesn’t
actually spend that much time with Kendall

00:15:32.770 --> 00:15:33.770
through the movie.

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:37.670
We see the personal effect of the trial on
her, and her romance with the kid from Sixth

00:15:37.670 --> 00:15:40.430
Sense, but we spend far more time with Bundy.

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:44.209
If they were trying to tell the movie from
the perspective of someone other than Bundy,

00:15:44.209 --> 00:15:47.269
it makes sense that we wouldn’t have seen
his crimes.

00:15:47.269 --> 00:15:49.520
Except that isn’t the story being told.

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:54.820
The majority is told from Bundy’s perspective
and Kendall’s so why DON’T we see his

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:55.820
murders?

00:15:55.820 --> 00:15:59.509
The movie tries to juggle these two perspectives
but doesn’t fully commit to either and as

00:15:59.509 --> 00:16:04.199
a result tells the story of a sexy MAN less
than a sexy serial killer.

00:16:04.199 --> 00:16:08.699
If the movie only wanted to give us a vision
of Ted Bundy as a pop figure, it did a great

00:16:08.699 --> 00:16:13.149
job, but if people come away from the movie
seeing him as more of a sex symbol than a

00:16:13.149 --> 00:16:15.770
mass murderer, is that okay?

00:16:15.770 --> 00:16:20.639
The 2002 Ted Bundy movie’s intent, on the
other hand, is to show a repugnant man for

00:16:20.639 --> 00:16:25.500
what he was, and it certainly does accomplish
that, but importantly, that doesn’t necessarily

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:28.380
make it a good movie.The movie is bad for...

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:29.459
Other reasons.

00:16:29.459 --> 00:16:34.130
In the end both movies fail but it's Extremely
Wicked that suffers in the most awkward, creepy

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:35.130
way.

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:39.339
Given the publicity surrounding its release
and the headline-friendly casting of Zac Efron,

00:16:39.339 --> 00:16:43.980
expectations were high and while we can see
what it was going for, its preoccupation with

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:47.770
its own aesthetics leaves the viewer wondering
“what was the point of that?”

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:51.580
What people think of as 'Beauty' comes with
its own set of standards like clean, straight

00:16:51.580 --> 00:16:56.600
lines, symmetry and a lack of blemishes, but
all these things require hiding or removing

00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:58.779
something deemed unattractive.

00:16:58.779 --> 00:17:04.130
The real Ted Bundy knew this and used it to
horrific effect by presenting himself as appealing

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:06.819
and hiding the worst parts of him.

00:17:06.819 --> 00:17:09.799
Netflix's movie does exactly the same thing.

00:17:09.799 --> 00:17:11.380
But what do you think Wisecrack?

00:17:11.380 --> 00:17:15.630
Is Efron’s Bundy worthy of another Teen
Choice award or should one of the most prolific

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:20.079
serial killers in American history be depicted
as ugly as he really was?

